---
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import { ARMS, REPS, SUMMARY_BY_ID, deskTasks, buildTasks, attempts, provenance, scorer, scorerDelta, REPO } from "@/lib/data";
const desk = deskTasks(), build = buildTasks(), led = attempts(), prov = provenance(), sc = scorer();
const term = ARMS.map((a) => {
  const rows = led.filter((r) => r.harness === a.id);
  const abnormal = rows.filter((r) => r.timed_out || (r.exit_code != null && r.exit_code !== 0)).length;
  const unknown = rows.filter((r) => !r.timed_out && r.exit_code == null).length;
  const acceptedAbnormal = rows.filter((r) => r.passed && (r.timed_out || (r.exit_code != null && r.exit_code !== 0))).length;
  return { a, attempts: rows.length, abnormal, unknown, acceptedAbnormal };
});
const delta = Object.fromEntries(ARMS.map((a) => [a.id, scorerDelta(a.id)]));
const [lead, other] = [...ARMS].sort((a, b) => SUMMARY_BY_ID[b.id].passed - SUMMARY_BY_ID[a.id].passed);
const frozenGap = SUMMARY_BY_ID[lead.id].passed - SUMMARY_BY_ID[other.id].passed;
const rawGap = SUMMARY_BY_ID[lead.id].raw_passed - SUMMARY_BY_ID[other.id].raw_passed;
const gapShift = 100 * (frozenGap - rawGap) / SUMMARY_BY_ID[lead.id].attempts;
const sections = [["reviewability", "Public evidence"], ["task-correctness", "Task validity"], ["grading-validity", "Evaluator sensitivity"], ["consistency", "Version and cohort limits"], ["elicitation", "Execution status"], ["quality", "Exposure and generalization"], ["roadmap", "What would resolve the gaps"]];
const desc = "Author self-audit of Business Bench: public evidence, retrospective scorer sensitivity, unavailable historical artifacts, task validity, exposure, and the studies needed for stronger claims.";
---
<Base title="Self-audit" description={desc} path="/audit">
  <nav slot="contents" class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><span class="toc-h">On this page</span>{sections.map(([id, label]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>)}</nav>
  <p class="meta">Author self-assessment<span class="sep">·</span>desk release <code>{prov.campaign}</code></p>
  <h1>Self-audit</h1>
  <p class="lede">The public record supports checking score accounting and inspecting the evaluation rules. It does not supply the private historical outputs or independent acceptance judgments needed to validate every verdict. This is the authors' evidence assessment, not an external certification.</p>
  <div class="callout warn">
    <p><strong>Conflict of interest and evaluator chronology.</strong> ERP.AI publishes the benchmark and develops Proto. The same organization constructed the primary frozen scorer after outputs existed. On those outputs, the observed lead changes from {rawGap} accepted attempts under the original evaluator to {frozenGap} under the frozen evaluator, a shift of {gapShift.toFixed(2)} percentage points. That is measurable evaluator sensitivity; its direction alone does not establish whether the revisions improve validity or introduce bias.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="reviewability">1. Public evidence and what it establishes</h2>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>Evidence</th><th>What a reader can check</th><th>What remains outside it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/results/latest/attempts.jsonl`} rel="noopener">Attempt ledger</a>, <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/results/latest/summary.json`} rel="noopener">summary</a>, <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/results/latest/provenance.json`} rel="noopener">provenance</a></td><td>Matrix completeness, verdict totals, per-check records, resource accounting, and the recorded cohort definitions.</td><td>Whether a historical output deserved its recorded verdict; hashes cannot reconstruct the output.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/${sc.dir}`} rel="noopener">Frozen scorer</a> and <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/${sc.dir}/manifest.json`} rel="noopener">manifest</a></td><td>The exact {sc.fileCount}-file package, copied task definitions, and equivalence rules for {sc.equivalenceTasks.length} tasks.</td><td>Whether the declared tests cover the right business properties or were selected independently of observed outputs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/tasks`} rel="noopener">Task packs</a></td><td>{desk.length} desk tasks and {build.length} build tasks: supplied inputs, requests, checks/checklists, generators, and references.</td><td>Representative business demand, independent practitioner agreement, or human completion times.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/harnesses`} rel="noopener">Adapters</a> and <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/results/latest/provenance.json`} rel="noopener">recorded configurations</a></td><td>Disclosed model routes, runtime identifiers, sampling settings where available, and current integration behavior.</td><td>A complete historical environment: private runtime binaries, image digests, authenticated skills, raw traces, and output workspaces are not fully distributed.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p><a href="/reproduce#public-accounting">Run the public accounting checks</a> without calling a model. The <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/bench/frozen_release.py`} rel="noopener">desk verifier</a> checks the ledger hash, recomputed summary, scorer fingerprint, and scorer identities. Original-result and receipt identities were checked against private source files during export, according to provenance; a fresh public clone cannot repeat that private-file comparison.</p>

  <h2 id="task-correctness">2. Task validity</h2>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Published evidence</th><th>Unresolved question</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Can the declared tests be satisfied?</td><td>The <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/docs/validation.md`} rel="noopener">release notice</a> reports reference-solution passes and untouched-workspace failures.</td><td>This establishes test-relative feasibility for those controls, not that an independent reader can infer every requirement from the request.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Are supplied inputs reproducible?</td><td>Repeated generation was checked under the validation environment. The notice lists 28 tasks with supplied bytes different from regenerated bytes.</td><td>Byte differences are not assumed harmless. Supplied release inputs remain authoritative; regenerated instances require their own identity.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Do tests agree with professional acceptance?</td><td>No independent practitioner agreement study or human completion baseline is published with this release.</td><td>Ambiguity, omitted requirements, plausible invalid outputs, and valid alternative representations need external adjudication.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2 id="grading-validity">3. Evaluator sensitivity is observable; error rates are not</h2>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <caption>Transitions computed from the released ledger on the same retained outputs. An original grader error is ungraded, rather than an ordinary agent failure.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>System</th><th class="n">Raw fail → pass</th><th class="n">Ungraded → pass</th><th class="n">Pass → fail</th><th class="n">Net accepted</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{ARMS.map((a) => <tr><td>{a.system}</td><td class="n">{delta[a.id].failToPass}</td><td class="n">{delta[a.id].ungradedToPass}</td><td class="n">{delta[a.id].passToFail}</td><td class="n">{delta[a.id].net > 0 ? "+" : ""}{delta[a.id].net}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>The additional {frozenGap - rawGap}-attempt difference is an evaluator change, not improvement from rerunning either agent. The frozen package was designed to admit specified alternative representations and enforce artifact checks. Without independent judgments on sampled outputs, the number of corrected false negatives and introduced false positives remains unknown.</p>
  <p>Native recalculation, readability checks, sentence-level predicates, and strict-validator controls address specific failure modes. They do not measure the residual false-acceptance or false-rejection rate. The process track has scripted negative controls, but those controls are not evidence that the historical desk scorer was comprehensively tested against plausible wrong answers.</p>
  <details><summary>Determinism, identity, and validity are separate</summary>
    <p><a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/results/latest/provenance.json`} rel="noopener">Provenance records</a> a 1,341-attempt scorer replay with zero verdict differences. That is a report about the replayed inputs and environment, not proof of determinism across every runtime or of agreement with business judgment. The immutable manifest preserves the scorer version; its digest is <code style="overflow-wrap:anywhere">{sc.manifestSha}</code>.</p>
  </details>

  <h2 id="consistency">4. Version and cohort limits</h2>
  <p>The two current cells share the reported task set and primary frozen scorer. Their models, harnesses, sampling controls, and cohort dates differ. Earlier snapshots and later development experiments are excluded in <a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/results/latest/provenance.json`} rel="noopener">provenance</a>; they should not be pooled into a single ranking or treated as controlled before-and-after changes.</p>
  <details><summary>Recorded source cohorts</summary>
    <ul>{ARMS.map((a) => <li><strong>{a.short}:</strong> {(prov.source_cohorts[a.id] as string[]).join("; ")}</li>)}</ul>
    <p>See <a href="/methods#cells">the configuration table</a>. Repetition labels are not common random seeds; the comparison did not randomize concurrent executions of otherwise identical systems.</p>
  </details>

  <h2 id="elicitation">5. Execution status does not certify elicitation</h2>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <caption>Known abnormal means a recorded timeout or a known nonzero exit code. Missing exit status is counted separately, not assumed to be zero.</caption>
    <thead><tr><th>System</th><th class="n">Attempts</th><th class="n">Known abnormal</th><th class="n">Unknown exit status</th><th class="n">Accepted with known abnormal exit</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{term.map(({ a, attempts, abnormal, unknown, acceptedAbnormal }) => <tr><td>{a.system}</td><td class="n">{attempts}</td><td class="n">{abnormal}</td><td class="n">{unknown}</td><td class="n">{acceptedAbnormal}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>These records reveal interrupted executions, but low interruption counts do not establish adequate prompting, budgets, tool access, or skill configuration. Budget sweeps and controlled configuration comparisons would be needed to measure sensitivity to those choices.</p>

  <h2 id="quality">6. Exposure, uncertainty, and generalization</h2>
  <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
    <thead><tr><th>Claim boundary</th><th>Current evidence</th><th>Stronger evidence needed</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr id="contamination"><td>Development exposure</td><td>The task set was used during development and is publicly inspectable. Synthetic generation does not establish absence of exposure.</td><td>Documented separation of development and evaluation inputs, sealed evaluation procedures, and independent review.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Repetition</td><td>{REPS} executions per supplied desk fixture. The same inputs recur; the paired task bootstrap retains repetitions together.</td><td>Additional seeds address new instances; held-out templates address a different boundary. Neither follows from repeating seed-identical inputs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Statistical uncertainty</td><td>A descriptive interval under empirical task resampling.</td><td>A justified workload sampling frame, treatment of related task families, and predeclared comparisons for broader inference.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Operational usefulness</td><td>Declared artifact acceptance and captured resource accounting.</td><td>Owner acceptance, failure severity, reversibility, review/repair costs, and deployment constraints.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Process and build transfer</td><td>A separately reported local-mode process pilot and released build task packs.</td><td>Track-specific controlled evaluations and completed application acceptance; desk scores do not supply them.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2 id="roadmap">What would resolve the evidence gaps</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Validate the contract.</strong> Independently review a stratified sample of requests, checks, and accepted and rejected outputs from both systems; publish the sampling rule, disagreements, adjudications, and uncertainty.</li>
    <li><strong>Probe evaluator failure.</strong> Test valid alternative representations and plausible invalid mutations such as identifier substitutions, duplicate settlements, or omitted authorization. Report false acceptance and rejection separately.</li>
    <li><strong>Separate sources of variation.</strong> Freeze the scorer before new outputs exist, use sealed instances and held-out templates, and run controlled configuration comparisons with declared repetitions and budgets.</li>
    <li><strong>Retain an inspectable experiment.</strong> Record task and image identities, runtime and skill inventories, sampling settings, and output retention/access conditions before execution.</li>
    <li><strong>Measure usefulness.</strong> Establish human completion and review effort, task ambiguity, and the relation between test acceptance and professional judgment.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>These are proposed studies, not completed assurances. See the <a href="/roadmap">roadmap</a> and <a href="/paper#8-validity-generalization-and-decisive-next-experiments">paper's validity discussion</a>. <a href={`${REPO}/issues`} rel="noopener">Report a task or grading dispute</a> with the release, task, check, and a reproducible counterexample. Changes to released tasks or scores require an explicit versioned correction.</p>
</Base>
